  "text": "“Mara still wants to come. Her work hours changed before the storm. She has to help the passengers off the last boat, and by then our gathering is over. She wrote this for you and asked me to bring it.”\n\nGrandma heard what had kept her friends away and shared The Empty Bench. The cushions were out again. Pip chose a real moment from the day for his own lantern, among the stories the garden already held.\n\nA section can reach the bank and still be loose. If you step onto an unsecured section, it may slip. Keep the secured part in place and recover the loose materials. You can use the same materials again.\n\nGrandma shared The Empty Bench, and Pip chose a real moment for his lantern. Afterward, he carried her story across the bridge to Mara. She took the copy at the dock to read when her work was finished.\n\nGrandma’s account says their visits had become less frequent before the storm. The storm damaged the bridge afterward. The friends needed both a way across the river and a way to share their stories.\n\n“I promised to have everyone’s bread ready today,” she said. “The people who ordered it will come whether my roof is ready or not. Help me keep this flour dry, and I can still get the dough started.”\n\nYou helped Sol repair the roof, watched Rina bake, and went with her to thank him. Sol’s draft stops before the baking and visit. Add the part you want him to tell. Write as Sol, using “I” for him.\n\nLook for a crossing where both sections together can reach the banks. Place the first section against the near bank. Seat its two near corner posts and its two center posts in their marked sockets.",
  "textSha256": "39b72ee3ceea5e0280a95da5e42b531c0138f15d9518007763ba4d5a2d4d09cb",
